cff-version: 1.2.0
title: A Shared Blind Spot in Numerical Quadrature
message: >-
  If you use this work, please cite it using the metadata
  from this file.
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Parsons
    given-names: Noah
    orcid: 'https://orcid.org/0009-0000-7224-6040'
repository-code: 'https://github.com/GuiloScion/shared-blindspot'
abstract: >-
  Differential testing catches the mistakes independent
  implementations make independently, and cannot catch the
  mistakes they make together. Five quadrature routines
  spanning three independent algorithm families were asked
  to integrate a narrow Gaussian bump with an elementary
  exact value. Below a width of 1e-3 all five return a wrong
  answer and report success; at the five narrowest widths
  every returned value is bit-identical, exactly 0.0, with
  four of the five claiming an absolute error of exactly
  0.0. Cross-implementation comparison detects nothing.
  Tightening tolerances never finds the bump, so the
  blindness follows from sampling at finitely many points
  rather than from any implementation defect. Falsification
  conditions were fixed before measurement.
keywords:
  - differential testing
  - numerical quadrature
  - silent failure
  - software verification
  - replication
license: MIT
